---
import GuidePage from '../../layouts/GuidePage.astro';
import Chip from '../../components/Chip.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import GoDeeper from '../../components/GoDeeper.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import Compare from '../../components/Compare.astro';
import CompareCol from '../../components/CompareCol.astro';
import CodeCaption from '../../components/CodeCaption.astro';
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
---

<GuidePage title='Server-Resolvable State - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 9 · Part A: Maximize the Known</div>
  <h1>Server-Resolvable State</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    The first render is a function of the request. Nothing more. State the request carries (the URL,
    route params, search params, cookies) is <em>known</em>; state living in a component's memory is
    invisible to the server, and every pixel that depends on it is unknown. This chapter is about
    putting state where the server can see it, which mostly means: use the URL more.
  </p>

  <h2 id='principle'>
    9.1 The principle: state the server can see is content the server can finish
  </h2>
  <p>
    When a request arrives, the server knows exactly four kinds of state: the
    <strong>path</strong> (which page, and its route params), the
    <strong>search params</strong> (the query string), the <strong>cookies</strong>, and the
    <strong>headers</strong>. That's the complete list. Everything the first render shows must be
    derived from those, so every piece of UI state you store anywhere else is a piece of UI the
    server cannot finish. <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    Watch what happens when a filtered table keeps its filter in <code>useState</code>. The server
    renders the page with… which rows? It doesn't know the filter: the state won't exist until a
    client component mounts and initializes it. So the table can't be server-rendered with real
    content; it becomes a client component that fetches after hydration, and that buys the entire
    Part B problem set (spinners, shifting, waterfalls; <a href='04-ssr.html#simplicity'>§4.2</a>)
    for a filter that would have fit in twelve characters of query string. Move the same state into
    the URL and the request
    <em>is</em> the state: the server reads <code>?status=active</code>, renders the right rows into
    HTML, and the "state management" is the platform's.
  </p>
  <p>
    The bonus features come free, and they're not small. State in the URL is
    <strong>shareable</strong> (paste the link, get the view),
    <strong>refresh-proof</strong> (F5 reproduces the page instead of resetting it),
    <strong>navigable</strong> (back/forward walk through filter history),
    <strong>deep-linkable</strong> (dashboards, emails, and docs can point at a specific view), and <strong
      >supportable</strong
    > ("send me the link" replaces "describe every dropdown you touched"). A view that lives in component
    state has none of these; it dies with the tab.
  </p>

