  <h2 id='url-state'>9.2 The URL is the state manager</h2>
  <p>
    What belongs in it:
    <strong
      >filters, search text, sort column and direction, pagination, tabs, view modes (table/grid),
      date ranges, expanded-group state on reports, selected item when it drives a panel</strong
    >; in short, anything that describes
    <em>which view of the data the user is looking at</em>.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The reading side is a server page prop; both are Promises in Next 16:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/reports/page.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportsPage</span>({ params, searchParams }: <span class="tok-fn">PageProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { programId } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { q = <span class="tok-str">""</span>, page = <span class="tok-str">"1"</span> } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> searchParams;

  <span class="tok-com">/* The URL fully determines this render. The same link produces the same
     page for anyone, the rows arrive as HTML, and there is no client-side
     state to hydrate, restore, or lose. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> reports = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getReports</span>(programId, { q, <span class="tok-prop">page</span>: <span class="tok-fn">Number</span>(page) });

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ReportTable</span> <span class="tok-prop">reports</span>={reports} /&gt;;
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The writing side you've already seen: an interactive leaf updates the query string with
    <code>router.replace()</code> inside a transition, and the server page re-renders with fresh rows:
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#refresh'>§5.8</a> has the search input, <a
      href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a
    > builds the full table around it, and
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#searchparams-boundary'>§5.6</a> covers the Suspense boundary
    the reading leaf must carry. Two writing details worth pinning down:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong
        ><code>replace</code> vs <code>push</code> is a product decision about the back button.</strong
      >
      Keystrokes and minor refinements should <code>replace</code> (back shouldn't step through every
      character typed); discrete view changes (a tab, a page number) can
      <code>push</code> so back walks the user's actual path.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reading a search param makes that part of the render request-time</strong>, which is
      exactly why it must sit behind a Suspense boundary under PPR (<a
        href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#searchparams-boundary'>§5.6</a
      >): the static shell prerenders, and the param-dependent region is a deliberate dynamic hole (<a
        href='04-ssr.html#page-cache'>§4.4</a
      >).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id='tabs'>9.3 Tabs are routes in disguise</h2>
  <p>
    The clearest instance of the whole principle, and the instinct most worth unlearning, is
    <strong>tabs held in <code>useState</code></strong>. A tab bar is a set of views with
    identities. "Which view am I looking at?" is precisely the kind of state the URL exists to
    answer, and holding it in memory forfeits everything at once: the link can't say which tab it
    points at, refresh snaps back to the default, the server can't render the active panel's
    content, and, since every panel must be reachable by a client-side click, <em>all</em> the panels
    ship to the client, rendered or not (<a href='07-first-render-js.html'>§7</a>).
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Tabs in state: the smell'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramView.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramView</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [tab, setTab] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-str">"reports"</span>);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabButton</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">active</span>={tab === <span class="tok-str">"reports"</span>}
        <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setTab</span>(<span class="tok-str">"reports"</span>)}
      &gt;
        Reports
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TabButton</span>&gt;
      {tab === <span class="tok-str">"reports"</span> &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Reports</span> /&gt;}
      {tab === <span class="tok-str">"roster"</span> &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Roster</span> /&gt;}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          View identity held in memory: not shareable, not refresh-proof, invisible to the server,
          and every panel ships to the client whether shown or not.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ Tabs as route segments'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">the route structure</span>app/
└── programs/
    └── [programId]/
        ├── <span class="tok-fn">layout.tsx</span>
        ├── reports/
        │   ├── <span class="tok-fn">page.tsx</span>
        │   ├── loading.tsx
        │   └── error.tsx
        ├── roster/
        │   ├── <span class="tok-fn">page.tsx</span>
        │   ├── loading.tsx
        │   └── error.tsx
        └── settings/
            ├── <span class="tok-fn">page.tsx</span>
            ├── loading.tsx
            └── error.tsx</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          The header and tab bar render once in <code>layout.tsx</code> and persist across tab changes
          (<a href='03-mental-model.html#rendering-model'>§3.1</a>). Each tab is a segment whose <code
            >page.tsx</code
          > is server-rendered, streamed, and prefetched by its Link, and each brings its own <code
            >loading.tsx</code
          > and <code>error.tsx</code> boundaries (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#streaming'
            >§11.2</a
          >).
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    With segments, each tab is a page: server-rendered with real content, prefetched by its
    <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> before the click (<a href='04-ssr.html#prefetching'>§4.5</a>), and
    addressable from anywhere. That leaves one real decision: <strong>where does the tab bar live,
    and how does it know which tab is active?</strong> There are four URL-backed approaches, and
    they are not equal. In order of preference:
  </p>

  <h3 id='tabs-page'>Approach 1: segments, with the bar in each page</h3>
  <p>
    The page already knows which page it is. So let each sibling page render the tab bar and
    <em>tell it</em> which tab is active, as a plain prop. The bar becomes a pure server component:
    no directive, no hook, no hydration, nothing read back from the URL at runtime, because the
    active tab is a static fact of the file it is rendered from.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/ProgramTabs.tsx - a server component; no directive, no hook</span><span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramTabs</span>({ programId, active }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span>; active: <span class="tok-fn">TabId</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabList</span>&gt;
      {TABS.map((tab) =&gt; (
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabLink</span>
          <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={tab.href}
          <span class="tok-prop">href</span>={<span class="tok-str">`/programs/${programId}/${tab.href}`</span>}
          <span class="tok-prop">active</span>={tab.href === active}
        &gt;
          {tab.label}
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TabLink</span>&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TabList</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/reports/page.tsx - the page declares its own identity</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportsPage</span>({ params }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { programId } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex min-h-0 grow flex-col"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramTabs</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} <span class="tok-prop">active</span>=<span class="tok-str">"reports"</span> /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Reports</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The benefit is purity: zero client JavaScript for the strip, and the active state never has
      to be computed because it was never unknown. The costs are two. The bar lives at page level,
      so it re-renders on every tab change (cheaply: it is a row of links, and the swap is
      invisible when the markup is identical), and every sibling page must remember to render it,
      which a shared server wrapper mitigates but does not delete.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='tabs-layout'>Approach 2: segments, with the bar in the layout</h3>
  <p>
    If the bar should be written once and <em>never repaint</em> across tab changes, it belongs in
    the (group) layout (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html#layouts'>§10.4</a>). That buys
    the strongest stability the router offers, and it costs exactly one small client boundary:
    layouts persist across navigations without re-rendering (<a
      href='03-mental-model.html#rendering-model'>§3.1</a
    >), so a server-rendered active state in a layout would go stale on the first click. Only a
    client component re-renders during navigation, and the platform ships a purpose-built hook for
    precisely this: <code>useSelectedLayoutSegment</code>, which reads the active route segment one
    level below the layout. Prefer it over <code>usePathname</code> string-matching: it is
    semantic, and it doesn't break when routes nest.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/layout.tsx - the bar is written once, in the persistent frame</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramLayout</span>({ children, params }: <span class="tok-fn">LayoutProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { programId } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex max-h-full flex-col overflow-hidden"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramTabs</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
      &lt;main <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"min-h-0 grow overflow-y-auto"</span>&gt;{children}&lt;/main&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The layout owns the header and the bar; each tab's <code>page.tsx</code> swaps in as <code
        >children</code
      > beneath them. Because the layout persists across sibling navigations, this markup renders
      once per visit, not once per tab change.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/ProgramTabs.tsx - the one client leaf a layout-resident bar needs</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useSelectedLayoutSegment } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/navigation"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramTabs</span>({ programId }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* The segment one level below the layout: "reports" | "roster" |
     "settings". Re-renders on navigation because this is a client leaf;
     the layout around it never does. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> segment = <span class="tok-fn">useSelectedLayoutSegment</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabList</span>&gt;
      {TABS.map((tab) =&gt; (
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabLink</span>
          <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={tab.href}
          <span class="tok-prop">href</span>={<span class="tok-str">`/programs/${programId}/${tab.href}`</span>}
          <span class="tok-prop">active</span>={tab.href === segment}
        &gt;
          {tab.label}
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TabLink</span>&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TabList</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Rendered once from the layout, persistent across every tab change, and the active indicator
      updates because this leaf (alone) re-renders. The trade against Approach 1 is one small
      client boundary and its hydration, purchased deliberately for the never-repaints guarantee.
      Between the two segment approaches the choice is taste and stakes: bar-in-page when zero
      client JS matters most, bar-in-layout when the persistent frame does.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='tabs-parallel'>Approach 3: a parallel-route slot, when tabs are one region among several</h3>
  <p>
    A third segment-based shape exists for a specific composition: the tabbed area is <em>part</em>
    of the page, sitting beside content that must not re-render when the tab changes. Make the
    tabbed region a <strong>parallel-route slot</strong> (<a
      href='13-blocking-requests.html#parallel-routes'>§13.3</a
    >) with its own sub-routes: the tab links are ordinary URLs, only the slot's subtree navigates,
    and every sibling region keeps its state untouched (<a
      href='13-blocking-requests.html#slot-subroutes'>§13.3</a
    >'s sub-navigation mechanics). Each tab is still a real segment, so the per-tab splitting,
    <code>loading.tsx</code>/<code>error.tsx</code>, and prefetching all survive.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/ - the tabbed region is a slot beside the main content</span>[programId]/
├── layout.tsx              <span class="tok-com">renders children AND the view slot</span>
├── page.tsx                <span class="tok-com">the always-present summary</span>
├── default.tsx             <span class="tok-com">children's fallback on hard loads (§13.3)</span>
└── @view/
    ├── reports/page.tsx    <span class="tok-com">/programs/[id]/reports</span>
    ├── roster/page.tsx     <span class="tok-com">/programs/[id]/roster</span>
    └── default.tsx</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/layout.tsx - the slot renders alongside the page</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramLayout</span>({ children, view, params }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { programId } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex max-h-full flex-col overflow-hidden"</span>&gt;
      {children}
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramTabs</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* The frame clips; it's up to the slot's children to implement scroll. */}</span>
      &lt;section <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"min-h-0 grow overflow-hidden"</span>&gt;{view}&lt;/section&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      A tab click navigates only the <code>@view</code> slot's subtree; the summary above it never
      re-renders and never loses state. Slots can also be rendered <em>conditionally</em> by the
      layout (<code>&#123;isAdmin ? adminView : view&#125;</code>), which is how role-dependent tab
      sets stay server-decided. The cost is ceremony: slot folders, <code>default.tsx</code> files
      for hard loads, and a structure worth reserving for pages where independent regions genuinely
      coexist; when the page <em>is</em> the tabs, Approaches 1 and 2 are simpler.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='tabs-param'>Approach 4: a query param (the last URL resort)</h3>
  <p>
    The fourth shape keeps one route and rides the tab on the query string: <code>?tab=roster</code>,
    with the server page switching on <code>searchParams</code>. It is still the URL, so it keeps
    the non-negotiables (shareable, refresh-proof, server-resolvable), which places it far above
    <code>useState</code>. But among the URL options it should be the <strong>deliberate last
    choice</strong> for tabs, because it discards everything the router grants to segments:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-warn'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/page.tsx - one route; the tab rides the query string</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* Recover code splitting by hand: each panel is its own chunk, loaded
   only when its tab is active. Segments would have done this for free. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> PANELS = {
  <span class="tok-prop">reports</span>: <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./panels/Reports"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.Reports)),
  <span class="tok-prop">roster</span>: <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./panels/Roster"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.Roster)),
  <span class="tok-prop">settings</span>: <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./panels/Settings"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.Settings)),
};

<span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramPage</span>({ params, searchParams }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { programId } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { tab = <span class="tok-str">"reports"</span> } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> searchParams;
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> Panel = PANELS[tab] ?? PANELS.reports;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex min-h-0 grow flex-col"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ViewTabs</span> <span class="tok-prop">active</span>={tab} /&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* Recover the loading boundary by hand: panels that fetch on the
          server suspend here instead of blocking the whole page. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">PanelSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Panel</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      If you take this road, take all of it: the panels must be <code>dynamic()</code> imports (or
      the inactive tabs' code ships anyway, <a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a>) and
      any panel that fetches server-side data must sit behind its own <code>Suspense</code> (or its
      fetch blocks the entire page's render). Notice what that adds up to: the page is hand-building
      the code loading, the boundary, and the switch that the file system provides for free, and it
      still has no per-tab <code>error.tsx</code> and no per-tab prefetch.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No per-tab code splitting.</strong> The router splits by route segment, and it cannot
      split what the URL doesn't distinguish: all three panels' code belongs to one page module and
      loads together (<a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a>). With segments, only the
      visited tab's panel ships.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No per-tab file conventions.</strong> Segments get their own <code>loading.tsx</code>
      and <code>error.tsx</code> for free; a query-param page hand-builds every boundary, and an
      error in one panel is contained only as well as you remembered to contain it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Coarser re-renders, weaker prefetch.</strong> A tab change re-renders the entire page
      segment on the server rather than swapping one child segment beneath a persistent parent. And
      <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> prefetches route segments: three tabs as segments are each
      prefetched before the click, while <code>?tab=</code> variants of one route are not
      prefetched apiece.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong semantics.</strong> Query parameters parameterize a view (sort, filter, page);
      tabs <em>are</em> views. A thing with an identity deserves an address, and downstream
      machinery (caching, analytics, error reporting) all keys more usefully on paths than on
      params.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where the param version is genuinely fine: toggle-like variations of one view that share the
    same data and nearly the same code, where separate folders would be ceremony; and embedded
    sub-switches too minor to deserve routes. When you do use it,
    <a href='#url-writes'>§9.4</a>'s transition-plus-optimistic recipe is what keeps it feeling
    native.
  </p>

